# Copy this file to .env.local and fill in the values from your Supabase
# project. .env.local is gitignored; this template is not, so never put a
# real value in here.
#
#   cp client/.env.local.example client/.env.local
#
# Where each value comes from (Supabase dashboard → your project):
#
#   VITE_SUPABASE_URL       shown on the project home page, directly under the
#                           project name, with a Copy button beside it.
#
#   VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY  Project Settings → API Keys → "Publishable key",
#                           which starts sb_publishable_. Older projects call
#                           the same thing "anon / public" on the Legacy tab;
#                           the client accepts either variable name, so
#                           VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY works too and reads
#                           more honestly on a new project.
#
# Supabase has moved off JWT-style keys. The legacy anon/service_role pair were
# JWTs signed with the project's JWT secret, so rotating a leaked one meant
# rotating that secret and logging every user out. The sb_publishable_ and
# sb_secret_ keys are opaque and revocable on their own, which is why the
# dashboard now lets you mint several and retire them one at a time.
#
# Both of these are meant to be public. Vite inlines anything prefixed VITE_
# into the browser bundle, so treat them as visible to every visitor. That is
# by design: the anon key only grants what your row-level security policies
# allow, which is why RLS has to be switched on for every table before this
# goes anywhere near production.
#
# The service_role / secret key is the opposite: it bypasses RLS entirely.
# It must never appear in this directory, never carry a VITE_ prefix, and
# never reach the client bundle.

VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-ref.supabase.co
VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your-anon-or-publishable-key
